1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,357
This is ice, near the equator.

2
00:00:02,682 --> 00:00:06,432
In 1988, a satellite saw it
capping the highest ridges

3
00:00:06,478 --> 00:00:08,139
of a mountain range in New Guinea.

4
00:00:08,464 --> 00:00:10,797
This is the same place in 2026.

5
00:00:11,122 --> 00:00:13,270
Only a few small fragments are left.

6
00:00:13,595 --> 00:00:17,949
NASA's Landsat satellites have watched
this ice for more than forty years.

7
00:00:18,391 --> 00:00:22,651
And scientists now say the last of it
could be gone within a few years.

8
00:00:23,162 --> 00:00:27,957
The mountain is Puncak Jaya,
in Indonesia's Sudirman Range,

9
00:00:28,085 --> 00:00:29,594
on the island of New Guinea.

10
00:00:30,035 --> 00:00:32,740
Its summit stands
about sixteen thousand feet high,

11
00:00:32,787 --> 00:00:34,621
close to forty-nine hundred meters.

12
00:00:34,830 --> 00:00:37,791
And it sits only about four
degrees south of the equator.

13
00:00:38,116 --> 00:00:42,400
Right next to it lies the largest
expanse of warm ocean water on Earth.

14
00:00:42,609 --> 00:00:45,918
That puts these glaciers near
the warmest conditions in which ice

15
00:00:45,953 --> 00:00:47,497
can survive all year.

16
00:00:47,776 --> 00:00:49,726
For thousands of years, it did.

17
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:52,594
These are false-color Landsat images.

18
00:00:52,919 --> 00:00:54,521
Ice shows up light blue.

19
00:00:54,765 --> 00:00:57,400
Bare rock is brown, and plants are green.

20
00:00:57,679 --> 00:01:02,369
In 1980, there were about six square
kilometers of ice near the highest peaks.

21
00:01:02,648 --> 00:01:06,386
By 1988, about four point
three square kilometers,

22
00:01:06,595 --> 00:01:08,790
roughly one point seven square miles.

23
00:01:09,034 --> 00:01:11,855
Then the pieces start
to vanish, one by one.

24
00:01:12,296 --> 00:01:16,313
That gray scar spreading
across the left side is the Grasberg mine.

25
00:01:16,754 --> 00:01:21,259
By September 2025, satellite
and aerial data showed only

26
00:01:21,317 --> 00:01:24,672
about zero point zero nine
square kilometers left.

27
00:01:25,183 --> 00:01:27,459
That's about a dozen soccer fields.

28
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:30,802
Roughly two percent
of what was there in 1988.

29
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:32,555
The ice got thinner too.

30
00:01:32,997 --> 00:01:37,292
At the main monitoring site,
a patch called the East Northwall Firn,

31
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:40,543
the ice was thirty-two
meters thick in 2010.

32
00:01:40,822 --> 00:01:42,772
That's about a hundred and five feet.

33
00:01:43,097 --> 00:01:45,919
By 2023, it was four meters.

34
00:01:46,244 --> 00:01:50,086
By November 2024, the ice
at that site was gone.

35
00:01:50,411 --> 00:01:52,966
As the glaciers thin, they break apart.

36
00:01:53,244 --> 00:01:56,402
And every new fragment adds
new edges where ice can melt.

37
00:01:56,611 --> 00:01:58,155
Snow still falls up here.

38
00:01:58,329 --> 00:02:02,242
But on bare rock, it doesn't last
long enough to build new ice.

39
00:02:03,194 --> 00:02:07,327
Chris Shuman, a retired glaciologist
who has tracked these glaciers

40
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:10,473
in satellite images, calls it
a house of cards problem.

41
00:02:10,856 --> 00:02:16,046
Once this process gets going, he says,
there's really no turning back.

42
00:02:16,255 --> 00:02:17,602
To count as a glacier,

43
00:02:17,776 --> 00:02:21,259
ice has to be thick enough to flow
downhill under its own weight.

44
00:02:21,642 --> 00:02:25,508
What's left here has broken
into small patches that no longer flow.

45
00:02:26,019 --> 00:02:30,466
Donaldi Permana, a climate scientist
at Indonesia's weather agency,

46
00:02:30,675 --> 00:02:34,436
says their life as active
glaciers is effectively over.

47
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:37,814
Recent satellite studies
suggest the main ice areas

48
00:02:37,873 --> 00:02:40,369
could disappear completely around 2030.

49
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:45,106
Estimates from Indonesia's weather agency
suggest it could happen much sooner,

50
00:02:45,315 --> 00:02:47,787
sped up by the current El Niño.

51
00:02:47,787 --> 00:02:48,635
Here's why.

52
00:02:49,447 --> 00:02:53,464
During El Niño, the air warms
and the freezing level rises above the summit.

53
00:02:53,592 --> 00:02:55,926
So storms bring rain instead of snow.

54
00:02:56,436 --> 00:03:00,616
With less fresh white snow to reflect
sunlight, the ice gets darker,

55
00:03:00,732 --> 00:03:02,961
absorbs more heat and melts faster.

56
00:03:03,472 --> 00:03:06,793
During the 2015 to 2016 El Niño,

57
00:03:07,071 --> 00:03:10,589
the ice thinned about five
times faster than usual.

58
00:03:10,914 --> 00:03:16,406
It lost roughly five point three meters,
about seventeen feet, in a single year.

59
00:03:17,102 --> 00:03:21,630
Scientists call tropical glaciers like
these canaries in the coal mine.

60
00:03:22,234 --> 00:03:27,505
Others are shrinking too:
the Quelccaya Ice Cap in Peru, the ice fields

61
00:03:27,551 --> 00:03:32,764
of Kilimanjaro in Tanzania, and what's
left of Humboldt Glacier in Venezuela.

62
00:03:33,461 --> 00:03:38,523
An aerial survey planned for December
should show how much ice is left this year.

63
00:03:38,767 --> 00:03:44,398
Until then, this Landsat image
from October 2026 shows what remains:

64
00:03:44,723 --> 00:03:46,905
a few blue fragments on bare rock.

65
00:03:47,289 --> 00:03:51,317
The El Niño speeding this up
is the same one NASA watched turn

66
00:03:51,364 --> 00:03:52,919
the Pacific red this year.

67
00:03:53,198 --> 00:03:55,079
Watch this one next.
